# pico-cam-a-platform

Waveshare PICO-Cam-A board, LCD and HM01B0 camera providers.

The original HM01B0 camera support code is ArduCAM's RPI-Pico-Cam:
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam

The upstream camera implementation and sensor initialization header are in
rp2040_hm01b0/arducam:
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam/tree/master/rp2040_hm01b0/arducam
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam/blob/master/rp2040_hm01b0/arducam/hm01b0_init.h

The upstream PIO program is rp2040_hm01b0/image.pio:
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam/blob/master/rp2040_hm01b0/image.pio

This integration used the HM01B0 table and camera example supplied in
Waveshare's PICO-Cam-A support archive, which adapts that ArduCAM code:
https://files.waveshare.com/wiki/PICO-Cam-A/PICO-Cam-A.zip

camera/hm01b0_init.h retains the original filename and the notice from the
Waveshare distribution. Its table is adapted for this integration: the
adapter performs software reset separately, the sentinel is omitted in favor
of the array length, and the QVGA window is enabled. The original gated,
MSB-first serial setting 0x3060=0x30 is retained. The Waveshare version's
other register settings are retained.

camera/capture.inc.c adapts the upstream PIO/DMA capture approach for this
board and the modules.cpp interface, adding timeouts, frame synchronization
in PIO, sensor identity checks, resource allocation, and cleanup. It is an
adaptation of the original support approach, not a claim of original camera
support authorship. The C++ provider and portable mm.camera interface are
integration code; the LCD panel settings come from Waveshare's LCD example.

## Camera host regression test

camera/test/capture.c was written by Codex during this board integration.
It is project test code, not code supplied by ArduCAM or Waveshare. It
provides a recording Pico SDK test double and directly includes
camera/capture.inc.c to exercise the production camera implementation on
the host, without the Pico SDK or camera hardware.

The test is C because capture.inc.c is compiled as C inside the Pico SDK
adapter. Direct inclusion checks that implementation under the same C
language rules; the public mm.camera interface and board provider are C++20
modules. camera/test/run.sh compiles the test as C11 with -Wall, -Wextra,
and -Werror, runs it from a temporary directory, and removes its output.
Set CC to select another host C compiler.

Run from the repository root:

```sh
sh boards/pico_cam_a/camera/test/run.sh
```

It checks invalid capture arguments, calls before initialization, clock and
resource constraints, PIO fallback, sensor identity and transport failures,
repeated initialization, capture timeout recovery, DMA configuration, FIFO
stall reporting, and resource cleanup even when sensor standby fails.

The simulated SDK does not validate real PIO timing, electrical wiring,
frame rate, or image quality. Those require testing on a PICO-Cam-A board.

## Live preview controls

apps/camera-demo starts paused. K3 (GP18) starts and stops capture; K4 (GP19)
cycles NEAREST, AREA averaging, and AREA+TIME averaging, including while
paused. Both buttons are active-low, watched with pull-ups and release
debouncing. The LCD shows state and mode; stopping retains the last image
and releases camera resources. Eight startup frames are discarded before
showing video. Temporal averaging resets on restart or mode change.

The preview processing, controls, and apps/camera-demo/test/preview.cpp are
new modules.cpp application/test code, not upstream camera support code.
Run the C++20 host test with sh apps/camera-demo/test/run.sh. It checks
weighted area sampling against a reference, temporal history, button
bounce, held buttons, taps latched during capture, and timer wraparound.
It does not measure image quality or verify physical button behavior.

## Capture timing audit

ArduCAM's image.pio waits for LVLD, waits for PCLK high, samples one bit,
and waits for PCLK low. Its ST7735 demo sets clk_sys to 250 MHz and uses
PWM wrap 9 for a 25 MHz MCLK, giving 10 PIO cycles per serial bit. Its base
example at 125 MHz uses a 12.5 MHz PWM MCLK, again 10 cycles per bit. The
PWM comment mentioning wrap 5 is stale; the executable code uses wrap 9.

PICO-Cam-A has a fixed 36 MHz oscillator, so GPIO PWM cannot slow its MCLK.
The previous three-instruction loop at 125 MHz had only 3.47 PIO cycles per
bit: WAIT-high recognition followed by IN could sample after the bit's
valid window (HM01B0 Figure 6.5). Instruction throughput alone was not a
valid timing guarantee. The previous 0x3060=0x20 setting also incorrectly
cleared msb_en; it is restored to ArduCAM/Waveshare's 0x30, as specified in
HM01B0 Table 6.6 and register 0x3060.

The capture loop now matches ArduCAM's LVLD-high, PCLK-high, IN, PCLK-low
sequence, with the existing PIO FVLD synchronization prefix. Camera-linked
applications set the RP2040 core regulator to 1.15 V, wait 10 ms, then set
clk_sys to 200 MHz before C++ initialization or application peripheral
configuration. Raspberry Pi's current RP2040 datasheet section 2.15.3
specifies 200 MHz operation at 1.15 V. This provides 5.56 PIO cycles per
36 MHz bit. The adapter refuses initialization or capture below 200 MHz.
The clock setup is linked only when the camera provider is reached, and
remains in effect when the camera sleeps. No runtime clock change occurs
on K3 presses. PIO allocation now requires six instructions.

camera/test/capture.c also checks the voltage/clock startup sequence and
interprets the production PIO instruction array against a gated 36 MHz
serial waveform: 250 phase offsets, full 324-pixel lines, blanking, and a
one-row FVLD advance. It checks that each bit is sampled once in order and
within its data window. This model assumes the datasheet waveform and equal
input synchronizer delays; it does not measure pin setup/hold, metastability,
oscillator duty cycle, or hardware signal integrity. Board verification is
still required. AREA+TIME can separately produce a shifted ghost of moving
objects because it blends current output with previous frames; NEAREST and
AREA do not blend frames.

